From 029ef2f45e62b3c606b18dfb232f59551124909b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florent Merlet <florentmerlet73@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:05:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] TIP 282 (4/7): assignment "=" and separator ";" in
 expressions

TIP 282, by Will Duquette and Don Porter: "name = value" assigns a
variable and is the assigned value; "x ; y" evaluates x then y, the
value of y. The left operand of "=" is a bareword, the name itself, or
any other operand, whose value is the name: {::ns::v} = 1, "A($i)" = 1,
$ref = 1, name(...) = 1. "=" groups right-to-left and binds looser than
"?:"; ";" binds looser than "=", tighter than ",". Empty statements
(";;", ";" before ")" or at the end, ":;") have the empty string as
value.

- ParseLexeme: "=" is ASSIGN, ";" is SEPARATOR.
- ParseExpr: a bareword followed by "=" (not "==") is a name
  (AssignFollows); ASSIGN is never constant.
- CompileExprTree: ";" pops its left value; "=" compiles to storeStk,
  or to storeScalar when the name is a compiled local of a procedure.
- ConvertTreeToTokens: empty statements.
- Tests: exprAssign.test; parseExpr-16.33, parseExpr-21.2. Docs: expr.n.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MrNWAEyXLqekovNBvbMZ4z
